---
import Docs from '../../../layouts/Docs.astro'
import Callout from 'web-design-system/astro/marketing/Callout.astro'
import CodeBlock from 'web-design-system/astro/product/CodeBlock.astro'
import TableScroll from '../../../components/atoms/TableScroll.astro'

const toc = [
  { label: 'Tiers', href: '#tiers' },
  { label: 'The trait', href: '#trait' },
  { label: 'Events', href: '#events' },
  { label: 'Turn ownership', href: '#turn' },
  { label: 'Capabilities', href: '#capabilities' },
  { label: 'Approvals', href: '#approvals' },
  { label: 'Failure modes', href: '#failures' },
  { label: 'Contract tests', href: '#tests' },
]

const tiers = [
  { tier: '1. Native adapter', mechanism: "The CLI's own machine protocol", events: 'Structured', approvals: 'Where offered', resume: 'Session/thread ID' },
  { tier: '2. ACP adapter', mechanism: 'ACP over stdio', events: 'Structured', approvals: 'Yes', resume: 'session/load where supported' },
  { tier: '3. Terminal wrapper', mechanism: 'PTY plus a bundled screen manifest', events: 'Coarse: working, waiting, exited', approvals: 'No', resume: 'No' },
  { tier: '4. Plain pane', mechanism: 'A terminal the user drives', events: 'None', approvals: 'No', resume: 'No' },
]

const trait = `trait AgentHarness {
    async fn start(&mut self, spec: SpawnSpec, roots: SessionRoots) -> Result<Capabilities>;
    async fn open_session(&mut self, resume: Option<SessionId>) -> Result<SessionId>;
    async fn prompt(&mut self, input: PromptInput) -> Result<()>;
    async fn cancel(&mut self) -> Result<()>;
    async fn respond(&mut self, request: ApprovalId, decision: Decision) -> Result<()>;
    async fn shutdown(&mut self) -> Result<()>;
}`
---

<Docs
  title="Adapter contracts"
  description="The tier model and the UI-free contract every agent integration implements."
  toc={toc}
>
  <p>
    Shepherd treats agent protocols as adapters, not identity. <code>libs/agent-harness</code> is a
    UI-free crate with one async trait and one event enum. It has no Tauri, GPUI or database types,
    so the UI never branches on a particular CLI.
  </p>

  <h2 id="tiers">Tiers</h2>
  <TableScroll label="Adapter tiers">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Tier</th><th>Mechanism</th><th>Events</th><th>Approvals</th><th>Resume</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {tiers.map((entry) => (
          <tr>
            <td>{entry.tier}</td>
            <td>{entry.mechanism}</td>
            <td>{entry.events}</td>
            <td>{entry.approvals}</td>
            <td>{entry.resume}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </TableScroll>
  <p>
    Shepherd prefers the richest tier the installed CLI supports; the user can force a lower one.
    The task and diagnostics show the negotiated tier, so a wrapper's coarse "waiting" state is never
    confused with a protocol-level approval.
  </p>

  <h2 id="trait">The trait</h2>
  <CodeBlock filename="libs/agent-harness" code={trait} showLineNumbers={false} />
  <p>
    A native adapter speaks Codex or OpenCode's protocol directly. The ACP adapter uses
    <code>libs/acp-client</code> over stdio and covers Claude Code and other ACP agents. A terminal
    wrapper uses a PTY and a screen manifest when no protocol exists. The plain-pane tier is the
    user-driven fallback.
  </p>

  <h2 id="events">Events</h2>
  <p>
    Adapters send <code>HarnessEvent</code> values over one bounded channel per session:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>TurnStarted</code>, <code>Delta</code> (message or thought), and <code>TurnFinished</code> with a reason.</li>
    <li><code>ToolCall</code> start, update and end, plus <code>Plan</code> and <code>Usage</code>.</li>
    <li><code>ApprovalRequested</code>, <code>ApprovalResolved</code>, <code>ModeChanged</code> and <code>SessionState</code>.</li>
    <li><code>Exited</code> when the process ends.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Native protocol events are mapped into the transcript types owned by the harness crate, so
    persistence, rendering and search are shared. Adapters never write to the store.
  </p>

  <h2 id="turn">Turn ownership</h2>
  <p>
    The core, not the adapter, owns turn boundaries and assembly. It starts a turn on
    <code>TurnStarted</code>, assembles deltas into messages or tool calls, persists only settled
    entries, and moves the task on <code>TurnFinished</code>. It never infers completion from idle
    time or process exit.
  </p>
  <p>
    The core assigns a per-session <code>seq</code> and a canonical item ID to every persisted entry.
    Adapter item IDs are mapped in memory so later updates land on the same entry, and ordering
    survives reconnects and mixed adapters.
  </p>

  <h2 id="capabilities">Capabilities</h2>
  <p>
    Every adapter declares capabilities instead of assuming them: streaming granularity, images,
    resource links, model picker, mode picker, resume, cancel, approval kinds, whether approvals carry
    patches, usage reporting and agent-created terminals. Capabilities are probed at connect and
    refined per session. Unknown defaults to false; a false capability hides the corresponding UI
    rather than showing a dead control.
  </p>
  <p>
    Shepherd saves the negotiated capabilities with the session. A legacy handle can reconnect and be
    probed again. If the adapter falls back, the task shows the reason.
  </p>

  <h2 id="approvals">Approvals</h2>
  <p>
    An approval request carries the tool, arguments, the paths the call touches and an optional
    patch. If the adapter supplies no patch, the core diffs the named paths inside the worktree. If
    there are no paths either, the card says the diff is unavailable and shows the arguments only.
  </p>
  <p>
    Safe reads and a visible local command list are auto-allowed. Other calls go to the agent's own
    review mode where one is exposed; if no judge is available, the core creates a user card. Session
    and project grants are scoped to the configured executable, arguments and launch environment, and
    are reviewable in Settings → Projects.
  </p>

  <h2 id="failures">Failure modes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Binary missing</strong> — the task does not start; the error row names the install hint.</li>
    <li><strong>Version too old</strong> — the adapter probes methods and refuses features the installed version does not support.</li>
    <li><strong>Auth required</strong> — the adapter reports the provider's auth state; Shepherd does not store a key.</li>
    <li><strong>Process exits mid-turn</strong> — the task fails or becomes interrupted; completion is never inferred.</li>
    <li><strong>Approval bridge missing</strong> — the task is labelled unreviewed and no approval is claimed.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="tests">Contract tests</h2>
  <p>
    Each adapter has recorded protocol fixtures for parsing, one live smoke against an installed CLI
    and explicit tests for binary missing, version too old, auth required and process exit mid-turn.
    The ACP fake agent is built under <code>test-support</code>. The
    <a href="/docs/contributors/test-fixtures">test fixtures</a> page explains the layers and the
    gated live commands.
  </p>
  <Callout tone="note" title="No UI coupling">
    Do not add a Tauri, GPUI or database type to the harness crate. If the UI needs a new state, add
    it to the event or capability contract and let the shell render it.
  </Callout>
</Docs>
